WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:13.250
Here on VISUALPOLITIK, we’ve already made several videos about how Venezuela is going

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:14.639
through its toughest times.

00:00:14.639 --> 00:00:19.070
However, we still needed to address one of
the most serious consequences of the Bolivarian

00:00:19.070 --> 00:00:24.930
revolution’s gigantic failure.

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:27.970
And there’s no doubt, no debate: Venezuela
is adrift.

00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:32.480
With every day that passes, life in this country
becomes harder and harder.

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:36.420
With some of the highest violence, poverty
and inflation indices in the world and with

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:41.239
a growing drug trafficking and organized crime
industry, many, many Venezuelans can’t find

00:00:41.239 --> 00:00:45.860
any alternative to putting some space between
themselves and their homeland-- that is, leaving

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:54.830
behind a country that’s falling apart.

00:00:54.830 --> 00:01:00.539
Well, in a situation like theirs, would any
of you have hope?

00:01:00.539 --> 00:01:03.020
I’d certainly try to escape.

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:06.700
And that’s exactly what more and more Venezuelans
are doing, especially the youngest ones.

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:11.320
Now... some of you may be wondering, ok, Simon,
but... how big is this problem really?

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:18.810
Well... see, according to the UN’s calculations,
from 2014 to June of 2018, around 2,300,000

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:20.780
Venezuelans have left the country.

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:25.420
And these numbers won’t stop growing anytime
soon.

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:36.909
In other words, the Bolivarian revolution
has caused the greatest exodus in the history

00:01:36.909 --> 00:01:40.400
of Latin America.

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:45.659
Listen up.

00:01:45.659 --> 00:01:50.920
(THE EXODUS)

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:53.900
Societies are entities in constant movement.

00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:58.950
Throughout history, migration has been continuous,
whether for economic or political reasons.

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:04.360
To find better opportunities, to escape from
a threat, or simply to escape bad living conditions,

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:09.090
people are continually moving from one place
to another.

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:12.900
However, an exodus is something quite different.

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:17.579
The term exodus refers to the mass emigration
of people who are forced to leave the place

00:02:17.579 --> 00:02:18.579
where they live.

00:02:18.579 --> 00:02:22.420
That is to say, it isn’t about people who
dream of a better life, but people who have

00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:24.100
to leave to survive.

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:28.919
In general, throughout history, the most common
causes have been political, racial or religious.

00:02:28.919 --> 00:02:32.820
Perhaps the most common type of exodus throughout
history has been that of people forced to

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:39.669
leave their homes because another stronger
group took over and expelled them violently.

00:02:39.669 --> 00:02:44.220
For example, there’s the exodus of the Armenian
people in 1915 when hundreds of thousands

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:48.100
of people were forced to escape from the Turkish
Empire, a process that later became known

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:49.920
as the "Armenian genocide".

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:53.510
Another case would be that of the tens of
thousands of Republicans who faced the threat

00:02:53.510 --> 00:02:58.170
of reprisal, torture and condemnation of all
kinds from the Francoists, and were forced

00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:00.900
to leave Spain after the end of the Spanish
Civil War.

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:05.250
Or what happened in the Second World War,
or with the fight for independence in African

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:07.919
and Asian countries in the 1960s.

00:03:07.919 --> 00:03:11.820
Well, in our times we’ve had two very clear
examples:

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:16.260
The first was a product of the Syrian Civil
War which, since its beginning in 2011, has

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:20.370
driven millions of people to move inside and
outside of its borders.

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:22.480
The second case is that of Venezuela.

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:27.730
And everything indicates that its exodus will
end up being much bigger than Syria’s.

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:33.250
“At this moment the Venezuelan migration
must be qualified as a forced migration, because

00:03:33.250 --> 00:03:36.199
it isn’t planned and it’s for survival.”

00:03:36.199 --> 00:03:42.010
Beatriz Borges, executive director of the
Justice and Peace Center.)

00:03:42.010 --> 00:03:45.040
Because folks, we need to understand what’s
happening.

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:48.880
Understand that the hundreds of thousands
of Venezuelans who are escaping... don’t

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:50.940
do it for pleasure, but out of necessity.

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:55.190
And, unlike the examples we’ve covered,
the Venezuelan exodus isn’t the result of

00:03:55.190 --> 00:04:00.680
a war or the occupation of a foreign force,
but of the hunger, fear and misery caused

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:03.509
by the Bolivarian revolution.

00:04:03.509 --> 00:04:10.759
See, according to the last Living Conditions
Survey, in 2018, 87% of Venezuelans live in

00:04:10.759 --> 00:04:14.310
poverty, and more than 60% in extreme poverty.

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:17.700
These are sub-Saharan African numbers.

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:23.430
The paradox is that this humanitarian catastrophe
is happening in one of the most important

00:04:23.430 --> 00:04:26.350
oil powers in the world.

00:04:26.350 --> 00:04:30.400
Folks, the situation is beginning to get very
serious.

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:34.180
For example due to a lack of medicine and
health equipment within the country, diseases

00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:39.440
such as measles, tuberculosis, and malaria,
diseases that had been eradicated in the country,

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:42.980
haven’t only re-emerged, in some cases they’ve
become real epidemics.

00:04:42.980 --> 00:04:44.790
And what’s more:

00:04:44.790 --> 00:04:52.220
“There are more than 100,000 HIV/AIDS patients
at risk due to lack of access to necessary

00:04:52.220 --> 00:04:53.220
medication”.

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:56.790
United Nations Office for the Coordination
of Humanitarian Affairs)

00:04:56.790 --> 00:05:00.430
And of course, the lack of medicine has lethal
consequences for the population.

00:05:00.430 --> 00:05:05.560
But that’s not all, Venezuela is experiencing
such a huge production and supply problem

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:09.540
that it’s becoming increasingly difficult
to acquire basic food products.

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:13.400
For example, a can of tuna can cost more than
a minimum wage salary.

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:14.400
Crazy!

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:18.630
That, folks, explains why according to data
from the United Nations, while Diosdado Cabello

00:05:18.630 --> 00:05:23.290
and the rest of the regime giants are growing
fat on lobster dinners, around 1 and a half

00:05:23.290 --> 00:05:31.900
million Venezuelans are currently suffering
from malnutrition.

00:05:31.900 --> 00:05:34.880
And then there’s the violence issue, which
is also no joke.

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:40.680
Homicide statistics, for example, are at levels
typical of a country in a civil war.

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:46.440
Check this out.

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:50.560
And then there’s of course, robberies, looting,
extortion and all the other crimes that have

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:53.100
made this country a very dangerous place to
live.

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:55.460
And the Bolivarian police don’t help.

00:05:55.460 --> 00:05:56.460
Listen up.

00:05:56.460 --> 00:06:03.720
“More than 40% of July’s crimes were committed
by police officers” Venezuelan Violence

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:04.720
Observatory)

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:08.930
So, faced with this scenario we can’t be
surprised that just in the last 3 years, 2

00:06:08.930 --> 00:06:13.630
million Venezuelans have left the country
by sea, air, and land.

00:06:13.630 --> 00:06:19.970
Surely, you’ve all seen the huge rows of
Venezuelans walking, step by step, towards

00:06:19.970 --> 00:06:20.970
the border.

00:06:20.970 --> 00:06:21.970
Because…

00:06:21.970 --> 00:06:26.340
most of those who flee on foot sometimes take
buses, cars, mules... but lately many have

00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:31.560
begun walking to reach Colombia, Brazil and
Ecuador first, and then maybe, from there,

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:33.330
Peru, Chile or Argentina.

00:06:33.330 --> 00:06:37.530
And of course this is by no means an easy
trip.

00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:41.090
The journey is hard not only because of how
tiring it is and because of what they leave

00:06:41.090 --> 00:06:44.990
behind – families, friends, their homes
– but in many cases, these refugees fall

00:06:44.990 --> 00:06:48.570
victim to fraudsters, thieves, etcetera, etcetera.

00:06:48.570 --> 00:06:52.870
Fortunately, so far, the other Latin American
countries have shown great solidarity and

00:06:52.870 --> 00:06:57.150
have allowed almost unrestricted entry to
the hundreds of thousands of Venezuelans.

00:06:57.150 --> 00:06:59.480
However, this could all change.

00:06:59.480 --> 00:07:03.480
Receiving so many people at once isn’t always
easy, especially if the recipients aren’t

00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:08.020
precisely rich countries.

00:07:08.020 --> 00:07:12.800
(A HUGE PRESSURE)

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:16.240
Folks, it's true.

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:19.590
Some Latin American countries are beginning
to feel a lot of pressure.

00:07:19.590 --> 00:07:24.340
The Venezuelan exodus is so great that in
some cases they’re facing real capacity

00:07:24.340 --> 00:07:25.340
problems.

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:28.630
For example, in the border state of Roraima,
Brazil, the number of Venezuelans attending

00:07:28.630 --> 00:07:37.280
medical centers has increased from 700 in
2014 to more than 50,000 in 2017.

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:43.140
Just in the first 3 months of 2018 the number
reached over 45,000.

00:07:43.140 --> 00:07:48.470
“Every day 700 or 800 Venezuelans go through
the border and that’s creating many problems

00:07:48.470 --> 00:07:51.460
in terms of vaccination and organization.”

00:07:51.460 --> 00:07:54.020
Michel Temer, President of Brazil)

00:07:54.020 --> 00:07:58.330
In Colombia the number of Venezuelans already
exceeds one million people, in Peru there

00:07:58.330 --> 00:08:02.430
are more than 400,000 and in Ecuador almost
a quarter of a million.

00:08:02.430 --> 00:08:07.919
The wave is so big that in 2018 so far, more
than 4,000 Venezuelans have arrived in Ecuador

00:08:07.919 --> 00:08:08.949
per day.

00:08:08.949 --> 00:08:13.280
Of course, many of them go on to other countries...
countries like Chile, which in the first 6

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:16.060
months, between January and June, received
almost 150,000 Venezuelans.

00:08:16.060 --> 00:08:21.770
All of this is causing the most affected countries
to begin establishing some measures to reduce

00:08:21.770 --> 00:08:24.919
this huge flow of people.

00:08:24.919 --> 00:08:28.460
The Brazilian government, for example, has
announced the deployment of the military at

00:08:28.460 --> 00:08:32.950
the border and has stated that it will consider
establishing quotas, that is, a maximum number

00:08:32.950 --> 00:08:34.760
of people who can enter the country.

00:08:34.760 --> 00:08:39.709
For their part, Ecuador and Peru have begun
to demand the presentation of passports...

00:08:39.709 --> 00:08:40.709
Why?

00:08:40.709 --> 00:08:44.650
Well, because it isn’t an easy document
to obtain in Nicolás Maduro's Venezuela...

00:08:44.650 --> 00:08:49.560
because of the bureaucratic labyrinth and
the simple fact that they lack all of the

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:51.460
paper needed to issue them.

00:08:51.460 --> 00:08:58.070
Well, due to that and because many officials
do business by selling them on the black market,

00:08:58.070 --> 00:09:02.230
where a simple passport, whose official price
is only 18.86 sovereign bolivars – about

00:09:02.230 --> 00:09:08.640
20 cents – can cost between 700 and 5,500
dollars... depending on how quickly you need

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:13.290
them or the money the buyer has.

00:09:13.290 --> 00:09:22.480
And for those who are lucky enough to already
have a passport, when it expires they can

00:09:22.480 --> 00:09:26.670
extend its validity for 2 more years with
a stamp – because they won’t get another

00:09:26.670 --> 00:09:33.680
passport – but that procedure, unless you
go through the black market... it is quite

00:09:33.680 --> 00:09:40.830
slow, and could take up to 3 months.

00:09:40.830 --> 00:09:42.670
Anyway, that's the way things are.

00:09:42.670 --> 00:09:46.640
For the first time, Latin America is facing
a serious problem with its borders.

00:09:46.640 --> 00:09:47.640
And it’s not an easy problem.

00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:52.080
On the one hand you can’t prevent Venezuelans
from coming in, it’s a humanitarian issue

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:55.220
and there’s still a huge margin of refugees
to citizens.

00:09:55.220 --> 00:09:59.770
But it’s evident that if things go on like
this, more and more people will try to escape

00:09:59.770 --> 00:10:02.320
the country... and that’s quite a challenge.

00:10:02.320 --> 00:10:06.860
Here every Latin American country needs to
work in a coordinated manner and receive support

00:10:06.860 --> 00:10:08.300
from the international community.

00:10:08.300 --> 00:10:12.900
Of course, to be fair, the effort and solidarity
shown by many of the region’s countries

00:10:12.900 --> 00:10:13.950
has been extraordinary.

00:10:13.950 --> 00:10:17.100
It’s been an example of Latin unity.

00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:18.320
By the way... one final note...

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:20.830
It isn’t only the common Venezuelan who’s
leaving.

00:10:20.830 --> 00:10:24.640
In recent years, many Venezuelans who made
a fortune during the Bolivarian revolution’s

00:10:24.640 --> 00:10:29.149
dark period, as well as the families of many
high-ranking officials, have left... of course,

00:10:29.149 --> 00:10:31.580
with a full wallet.

00:10:31.580 --> 00:10:35.770
“Ex-Chavez ministers hid 2 billion in Andorra.”

00:10:35.770 --> 00:10:37.010
El País)

00:10:37.010 --> 00:10:40.230
“The opulent disembarkation in Venezuela
of the Venezuelan millionaires.

00:10:40.230 --> 00:10:46.690
A dozen businessmen and former officials,
several of them arrested for corruption, have

00:10:46.690 --> 00:10:50.700
invested tens of millions in luxury homes
and hunting lands.”

00:10:50.700 --> 00:10:51.700
El País)

00:10:51.700 --> 00:10:53.210
These are known as the Bolichicos [BO-LEE-CHEE-COS].

00:10:53.210 --> 00:10:55.320
We’ll try to talk about them in a future
video.

00:10:55.320 --> 00:10:58.770
But now it’s your turn, do you think that
the international community should help the

00:10:58.770 --> 00:11:00.810
Venezuelan people more?

00:11:00.810 --> 00:11:03.630
Leave your answer in the comments as well
as in the survey.

